$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: Preparation of the Resin-Bound Alkynes 2. The
MicroKanTM reactors, preloaded with the Rink amide resin 1 and an encoded
Rf tag, were treated with a solution of 20% piperidine in DMF for 1 h at
room temperature and then washed with DMF and DCM. The washing procedure
was repeated five times and the resin was dried overnight in vacuo.
Alkynoic acid (5.0 eq) was coupled to the resin using DIC and HOBt (5.0
eq each) in DMF-DCM (1:1) at room temperature for 1 h followed by washing
with DMF and DCM (x 5 each). The resin-bound 2 was dried overnight in
vacuo. Preparation of the Resin-Bound 2-Alkynylanilides 3. The
resin-bound alkynes 2 (20 reactors, 0.42 mmol) were charged in a Schlenk
flask along with the aryl triflate (748.2 mg, 2.10 mmol), Pd(PPh3)4 (48.5
mg, 4.20 x 10-2 mmol), CuI (16.0 mg, 8.40 x 10-2 mmol) and n-Bu4NI (232.7
mg, 0.63 mmol). The flask was evacuated and backfilled with N2 gas.
DMF-Et3N (5:1 v/v, 30.0 mL) was added via a syringe. After shaking at
room temperature for 24 h the reactors were then washed with DMF, MeOH
and DCM. The washing procedure was repeated five times. The resin-bound
2-alkynylanilides 3 were dried overnight in vacuo. Resin-free samples of
the 2-alkynylanilides 3 were obtained in quantitative yield after
cleavage from the resin. Preparation of Sulfonamides 6. Representative
Procedure for 6 (Ar = p-CF3C6H4). To the resin-bound 4-nitrobenzamides 3
(10 reactors, 0.21 mmol) were added a solution of SnCl2·2H2O in NMP (1.0
M, 12.0 mL), and the mixture was shaken for 24 h at room temperature. The
reactors were rinsed with DMF (x 5) and DCM (x 5). After drying the
resin-bound 4-aminobenzamides 5 in DCM-pyridine (5:1, v/v) were treated
with 4-(trifluoromethyl)-benzenesulfonyl chloride (0.16 mL, 1.05 mmol).
The reaction was shaken at room temperature for 24 h, and the formed
resin-bound sulfonamides 6 were washed with DMF and DCM (x 5 each) and
dried overnight in vacuo. Resin-free samples of the sulfonamides 6 were
obtained in quantitative yield after cleavage from the resin.
Microwave-Assisted Cyclization of the Resin-Bound Sulfonamides 6.
Representative Procedure for Synthesis of Indole 7 (Ar = m-CF3C6H4, n =
2). To an EmrysTM process 5-mL vial was added the resin-bound sulfonamide
6 (Ar = m- CF3C6H4, n = 2; 2.1 x 10-2 mmol) transferred from the
MicroKanTM reactor together with the Rf tag (Caution: The encoded Rf tag
cannot be archived after microwave irradiation if a 2-mL process vial is
used. Photos of the reaction vials are given in Figure 1.). To the above
vial were added Cu(OAc)2 (3.8 mg, 2.1 x 10-2 mmol) and dry NMP (1.5 mL).
The vial was then put into the microwave cavity on the EmrysTM creator
from Personal chemistry AB (Uppsala, Sweden) and heated at 200 °C for 10
min. The reaction mixture was transferred back to an empty MicroKanTM
reactor and the solvent was drained. The resin-bound indole 7 was washed
with DMF, MeOH and DCM, and dried in vacuo. After cleavage from the
resin. The indole 7 (Ar = m-CF3C6H4, n = 2) (8.3 mg, 82% from 1) was
obtained in 98% purity as determined by LC–MS analysis (Figure 1). 7 (Ar
= o-CF3C6H4, n = 2) 1H NMR (300 MHz, CD3OD) δ 8.19 (d, J = 7.62 Hz, 1 H),
8.10 (d, J = 7.23 Hz, 1 H), 7.90 (d, J = 8.79 Hz, 1 H), 7.89 (t, J = 6.81
Hz, 1 H), 7.81 (td, J = 7.71, 0.96 Hz, 1 H), 7.42 (d, J = 2.16 Hz, 1 H),
7.18 (dd, J = 8.94, 2.25 Hz, 1 H), 6.58 (s, 1 H), 3.47 (t, J = 7.32 Hz, 2
H), 3.21 (t, J = 7.14 Hz, 2 H), 2.81 (t, J = 7.38 Hz, 2 H), 2.01 (sextet,
J = 7.29 Hz, 2 H), 1.23 (t, J = 7.35 Hz, 3 H).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM microwave irradiation (sixth stage in Emrys creator app.),
solid-supported reaction, first stage is deprotection of rink amide
resin, second stage is attachment to resin, washing occur after each
stage, alternate conditions gave lower yield, sixth stage is key step
